WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.140
Did you guys know that Facebook has their
own space program? Nowadays, it seems like

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.890
every mega billionaire has their eyes on space.
Zuckerberg has actually been in the space

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:14.300
scene for a decent amount of time now having
started Facebook’s space program in 2014.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:20.530
But unfortunately, like Blue Origin, it doesn’t
seem like Zuckerberg has much to show after

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:26.841
several years of work. So, what happened to
Zuckerberg’s space program? Well, unlike

00:00:26.841 --> 00:00:30.570
the traditional space companies that we are
used to, it doesn’t seem like Facebook has

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.249
any ambitions to actually build and launch
a rocket themselves. Facebook is more focused

00:00:35.249 --> 00:00:39.671
on what can be accomplished using rockets
as opposed to the rockets themselves. This

00:00:39.671 --> 00:00:44.789
focus has led to three primary projects which
are a drone based internet service, a satellite

00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:48.989
based internet service, and a space probe
capable of traveling at 1/5th the speed of

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:52.839
light. Starting with the drone based internet
service, Facebook initially announced this

00:00:52.839 --> 00:00:58.350
project in 2014 in conjunction with their
new connectivity lab. The connectivity lab

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:03.309
was created by hiring ex nasa and ascenta
employees, and the lab was tasked with working

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:08.460
on the internet.org project. This project
was a partnership between 7 major corporations

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:15.479
including Facebook, Samsung, Ericsson, MediaTek,
Nokia, Opera, and Qualcomm. The goal of the

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:19.159
project was to bring affordable internet access
to the 5 billion people around the world who

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:23.670
didn’t have it at the time. Each company
in the partnership had a specific role within

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.930
the project. The smartphone companies had
the task of implementing special antennas

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:32.450
into their smartphones that could access the
new internet service. The other tech companies

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.880
had the role of improving data compression
by making it cheaper and more efficient. And

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.790
Facebook had the role of designing and launching
the internet drones themselves. This internet.org

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:46.009
project was eerily similar to Google’s Project
Loon. The only difference between the two

00:01:46.009 --> 00:01:50.140
projects is that Facebook proposed to use
drones while Google proposed to use a network

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.060
of balloons. Both of these solutions sound
super cool in theory, but they also seem super

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.960
impractical and pointless. Why were these
companies messing with drones and balloons

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.880
when they could just launch satellites to
provide internet service? Satellites can leverage

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.210
the gravitational force of the earth to basically
infinitely orbit around the earth with no

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:12.790
effort from the satellite. Drones on the other
hand have to constantly rotate their blades

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:16.890
to maintain altitude. While the latter option
is a much more inefficient solution, it shines

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.360
in two areas which are cost and accessibility.
Launching satellites is extremely expensive.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.070
Even for SpaceX, each satellite ends up costing
hundreds of thousands of dollars. Not to mention

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:29.710
the cost of the rocket launch. Meanwhile,
flying drones will likely only cost thousands

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:34.630
of dollars each. If anything, it might cost
tens of thousands which is still only a fraction

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:38.850
of the cost of launching a satellite. Aside
from being much cheaper, the internet service

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:44.200
offered by the drone network will be accessible
by significantly more devices. Nowadays, smartphones

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.670
are the easiest and cheapest way to access
the internet. Because of this, entry level

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:53.150
internet users especially in third world countries
are much more likely to have a smartphone

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:58.680
than a computer. So, providing mobile internet
service is more effective than broadband service

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.250
when trying to reach individuals with no internet
access. And, here’s the thing, even the

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:08.180
top flagship phones do not have the ability
to communicate with satellites in space. So,

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.840
it would be foolish to think that people in
3rd world countries have smartphones that

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.380
can access satellite internet. Considering
all of this, the reason facebook was launching

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.210
drones instead of satellites is because drone
based internet would provide internet that

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:25.670
is much cheaper and much more accessible to
the lowest tier of internet users. Unfortunately

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:31.370
though, this ambition wouldn't quite pan out.
In the summer of 2016, Facebook would conduct

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:36.090
the first flight test of their solar powered
drone. This test was actually a massive success

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.710
and was quite promising. The test flight was
only supposed to last 30 minutes, but it would

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:45.310
end up flying for 90 minutes. Now, the goal
was to have the drone eventually fly 90 days

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:50.980
at a time, but this was a great start. The
same cannot be said about the landing though.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:55.170
A strong gust of wind combined with software
issues would lead to one of the wings scraping

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:59.680
on the ground and a 20 foot section breaking
off. Facebook would end up correcting this

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.930
mistake in their second test flight, which
nailed the landing. But for some reason, Facebook

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:08.760
would decide to scrap the drone altogether
and shut down their manufacturing plant in

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.830
2018. This doesn't mean that Facebook gave
up on drone based internet though. They simply

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:17.320
ditched trying to make their own drone. Instead,
they would partner with Airbus in November

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.600
of 2017. Since then, we have heard about a
couple of test flights here and there, but

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:27.190
aside from that, there really hasn’t been
many progress updates. It’s assumed that

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:31.220
the companies are perfecting the drone and
if things go well, we could see a beta roll

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.781
out within the next couple of years. Moving
on, we have Facebook’s plans with satellite

00:04:35.781 --> 00:04:41.640
internet. This sector really needs no explanation.
With satellite internet, Facebook is looking

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.140
to provide broadband internet service to second
tier internet users. These people usually

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.660
already have broadband internet or at least
mobile internet. But, their connection isn’t

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:56.380
the best, so Facebook along with SpaceX, Blue
Origin, and OneWeb are looking to provide

00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:01.220
cheaper, faster, and more reliable internet
access to these people. Since Facebook doesn’t

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.870
have launch capability though, they have to
rely on rocket companies to launch their satellites.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:10.540
Ironically, one of their first satellite launches
was in conjunction with SpaceX, and this launch

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:15.730
happened to be one of SpaceX’s only failures.
Since then though, Facebook has successfully

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:20.080
built up a constellation of 53 small satellites,
and they hope to launch a broadband service

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.620
in the imminent future. I’m not too sure
how successful such a service will be though.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.620
Facebook will likely have a tough time matching
the prices offered by Starlink since they

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.540
don’t launch their own satellites. Facebook
also doesn’t have a massive subscriber base

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:38.020
that they can leverage. For instance, it looks
like Jeff Bezos and Amazon will be late to

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:42.330
the satellite game, but when they do enter,
it looks like they're positioned to become

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.790
the second largest satellite internet provider.
Not only would Jeff Bezos have launch capability

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:51.650
with Blue Origin, but he can also leverage
his massive prime subscriber base. I feel

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.870
like people would either just go with SpaceX
to support their mission or just go with Amazon

00:05:55.870 --> 00:06:00.110
because it’s convenient. So, I’m not quite
sure why someone would choose Facebook or

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:05.139
oneweb over the competition. But that’s
just what I think, let me know if you guys

00:06:05.139 --> 00:06:10.260
disagree. Anyway, that brings us into Facebook’s
final and likely their most exciting project

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.790
called Breakthrough Starshot. Zuckerberg founded
this project in April of 2016 with Stephen

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:19.300
Hawking and russian billionaire yuri milner.
The goal of the project is to send probes

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:23.180
to planets with liquid water. Planets with
liquid water are often called Goldilocks Zone

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:27.680
Planets because the fact that the water is
liquid proves that the planet is not too cold

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.280
or too hot. According to Zuckerberg, planets
that don’t have liquid water aren’t that

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.590
useful for human inhabitation. He previously
wrote quote “Mars has no water, so it would

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:41.770
be difficult to ever live there." Elon Musk
and his fans would likely disagree with this

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:46.320
notion, but it is true that terraforming Mars
into an Earth like planet would take thousands

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.530
and thousands of years. Zuckerberg claims
that "It's quite possible the closest planet

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:55.380
that humans could actually live on is orbiting
Alpha Centauri, and the only way to know that

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:59.590
for sure is to visit close enough to photograph
the planet, which is what this project will

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:05.060
do." The only problem with this idea is that
alpha centauri is 4.3 light years away or

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:10.610
about 25 trillion miles away. Even if we use
the fastest spacecrafts we have today, it

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.990
would take 30,000 years to get there according
to Zuckerberg. But, one of the main reasons

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:19.440
for such a long travel time is the large mass
of the spacecraft. Newton’s basic second

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.440
law states that force equals mass times acceleration.
So, if you decrease the mass substantially,

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:28.950
you can significantly increase the acceleration
and get much closer to the speed of light.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:33.560
And this is precisely what Zuckerberg plans
to do. Advancing technology has made it possible

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:38.690
to manufacture extremely small cameras and
sensors. And Zuckerberg wants to build nanoprobes

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:43.290
called nanocraft. As the name suggests, a
nanoprobe is an optical device that measures

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:48.790
as little as just 100 nm in length. To put
that in perspective, you could fit 100 million

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:53.090
nanoprobes within a single square millimeter.
Now, it’s unlikely that Zuckerberg’s nanocraft

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:58.570
will be that small, but that gives you a reference
as to how small nanoprobes can get. Given

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:02.370
the extremely small mass of such devices,
Zuckerberg is hoping to launch these probes

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:06.370
using a laser beam that will launch these
probes at ⅕ of the speed of light. That’s

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:12.160
the same as about 134 million miles per hour
or 60 million meters per second. Even at that

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:16.380
speed though, it’s going to take quite a
bit of time to actually reach Alpha Centauri.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:20.580
Zuckerberg expects that his nanocraft can
reach Alpha Centauri within 20 years after

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:25.610
launch. And once there, the nanocraft should
be able to identify any signs of liquid water

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:30.270
and extraterrestrial life on the planet. At
the end of the day, it’s no question that

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:34.819
space is the next big venue of growth, so
it’s not surprising that billionaires are

00:08:34.819 --> 00:08:39.629
crowding into the space. So far, Zuckerberg
has been working with Airbus to deploy cheap

00:08:39.629 --> 00:08:43.240
mobile internet world wide and rocket companies
to deploy cheap broadband internet worldwide.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.999
Aside from this, Zuckerberg is determined
to find the closest signs of liquid water

00:08:46.999 --> 00:08:51.740
and alien life with his nanocraft. Despite
the setbacks in all of these projects, Zuckerberg

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:56.139
has continued to persist forward. But only
time will tell which one of these endeavors

00:08:56.139 --> 00:09:01.980
if any will evolve into something much bigger.
Do you guys think Zuckerberg has a real chance?

